<Base
  title="About"
  description="What this site investigates, what kinds of writing belong here, and the standards behind them."
>
  <article class="prose container--narrow">
    <h1>About this site</h1>

    <p>
      This is a site about hidden structure — the mechanisms, tradeoffs, and patterns
      that explain why things work the way they do. Every piece begins with a question
      of the same kind: <em>what is actually happening underneath?</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      The subjects vary. The mode of inquiry does not. A piece might examine a software
      abstraction, a decision under constraint, a craft technique, or a cognitive pattern.
      What connects them is not topic but method: find the operational logic, name the
      real costs, make the structure visible.
    </p>

    <h2>What kinds of writing belong here</h2>

    <p>
      Three forms, each with a different purpose and bar to clear:
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Essays</strong> are complete explorations. A question is posed, examined, and
      taken somewhere. The reader arrives at a model, a reframe, or a sharpened understanding.
      An essay that doesn't go anywhere isn't ready.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Notes</strong> are compressed observations. A single mechanism noticed, a
      single pattern named, a single thing worth knowing precisely stated. Not a shorter
      essay — a deliberately minimal piece that doesn't need more than it has.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Syntheses</strong> make explicit connections between prior pieces or ideas.
      Not summaries — they identify the deeper pattern that only becomes visible when
      two or more things are held together at once.
    </p>

    <h2>Standards</h2>

    <p>
      Every piece distinguishes between what was observed, what was inferred, and what is
      speculation. Length is treated as a constraint, not a signal of effort. A piece is
      ready when it has said what it needed to say — not before, not after.
      The archive is meant to be useful to a reader who has never met the author.
    </p>

    <h2>Who writes here</h2>

    <p>
      Abhiraj D. This site exists because writing is how I build models I can actually use —
      and because models built in public tend to be sharper than models built in private.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
